title: 'For Terry: under a parent the user does not own, a child marked for deletion that carries ''(overridesHere N)'' still stands for N in the parent''s contains. In your ''local_fieldintent_collection'', since 2026-11-15.'
pid: 0273087c-43a4-4b70-a2c5-e14819506819
body: |2-
  'content_members' in 'traverse.rs' takes such a child as N when the
  parent occurrence's home skgrepo is not owned, by the predicate
  'deleted_overrider_keeps_its_original' in 'predicates.rs'. So
  deleting a drawn overrider under another's note no longer edits that
  note's contains, and the save no longer asks to fork it; only a
  removed headline does. Under an owned parent, and in
  'visible_content_members' (a subscriber's hides), nothing changed.
  For this the traversal takes the user's ownership of skgrepos as a
  parameter, 'IsOwned', threaded through every visit function; its
  callers pass 'SkgConfig::skgrepo_is_owned', and the unit tests in
  'tests/new/local_fieldintent_collection/' pass '&|_| true', all owned,
  which keeps their old meaning. Filing
  ('server/from_text/file_what_is_written_in.rs') still asks
  'occurrence_child_counts_as_content', so such a deletion files
  nothing. 'claiming_children_have_distinct_gnids' already counted the
  child as N, so N beside its own deleted overrider under one parent is
  refused as a duplicate, as before. Sue's mind built it at Top's
  request, from Ed's finding, because the fault showed in forks. The
  tests are in 'tests/override_substitution.rs' (grouped_overrides):
  the two functions whose names begin 'deleting_the_users_fork' and
  'a_deleted_overrider', and the last block of
  'extraction_honors_overridesHere', which pins the subscribee case.
